{"slug":"complex","pages":[{"names":["real","imag"],"summary":"The real and imaginary parts of complex numbers.","body":["For a real array, `real` gives it back and `imag` gives zeros. Together with `abs` and `angle` they take complex numbers apart; `complex` puts them together."],"syntax":[["x = real(z)","The real parts."],["y = imag(z)","The imaginary parts."]],"inputs":[],"outputs":[],"examples":[{"title":"The parts of some numbers","intro":"","code":"z = [3+4i, -2i, 5];\nreal(z)\nimag(z)","after":"","output":"ans =\n\n     3     0     5\n\nans =\n\n     4    -2     0","figures":[]},{"title":"The roots of a quadratic, split into parts","intro":"","code":"r = roots([1 2 5]);\n[real(r) imag(r)]","after":"","output":"ans =\n\n    -1     2\n    -1    -2","figures":[]}],"notes":[],"see":["abs","angle","complex","conj"]},{"names":["conj"],"summary":"The complex conjugate.","body":["The imaginary part changes sign: `conj(a + bi)` is `a - bi`. `z.*conj(z)` is the squared modulus, and the conjugate transpose `A'` conjugates too."],"syntax":[["w = conj(z)","The conjugate of each element."]],"inputs":[],"outputs":[],"examples":[{"title":"Conjugates and the squared modulus","intro":"","code":"z = 3 + 4i;\nconj(z)\nz * conj(z)","after":"","output":"ans =\n\n   3.0000 - 4.0000i\n\nans =\n\n    25","figures":[]}],"notes":[],"see":["real","imag","ctranspose"]},{"names":["angle"],"summary":"The phase angle of a complex number, in radians between -pi and pi.","body":["The angle from the positive real axis to the number in the complex plane: `angle(z)` is `atan2(imag(z), real(z))`. With `abs`, it gives the polar form `z = abs(z)*exp(1i*angle(z))`."],"syntax":[["theta = angle(z)","The phase of each element."]],"inputs":[],"outputs":[],"examples":[{"title":"Phases, and the polar form back","intro":"","code":"z = [1, 1i, -1, -1i, 1+1i];\nangle(z)\nw = 2 - 2i;\nabs(w) * exp(1i*angle(w))","after":"","output":"ans =\n\n         0    1.5708    3.1416   -1.5708    0.7854\n\nans =\n\n   2.0000 - 2.0000i","figures":[]}],"notes":[],"see":["abs","atan2","unwrap"]},{"names":["complex"],"summary":"A complex number from its real and imaginary parts (kept complex when the imaginary part is 0).","body":["`complex(a, b)` is `a + b*1i`, except that the result stays complex even when `b` is zero, where `a + 0i` would be stored as real. `complex(a)` makes a real array complex."],"syntax":[["z = complex(a, b)","Real parts `a`, imaginary parts `b`."],["z = complex(a)","`a` as complex numbers with zero imaginary parts."]],"inputs":[],"outputs":[],"examples":[{"title":"From parts, and kept complex","intro":"","code":"z = complex([1 2], [3 4])\nw = complex(5, 0);\nisreal(w)\nisreal(5 + 0i)","after":"","output":"z =\n\n   1.0000 + 3.0000i   2.0000 + 4.0000i\n\nans =\n\n  logical\n\n   0\n\nans =\n\n  logical\n\n   1","figures":[]}],"notes":[],"see":["real","imag","isreal"]},{"names":["isreal"],"summary":"True when an array is stored without imaginary parts.","body":["It tests how the array is stored, not its values: `complex(5, 0)` is not real by this test though its imaginary part is zero. Arithmetic that gives zero imaginary parts usually gives a real result."],"syntax":[["tf = isreal(A)","True when `A` has no imaginary parts."]],"inputs":[],"outputs":[],"examples":[{"title":"Stored as real or as complex","intro":"","code":"isreal([1 2 3])\nisreal(sqrt(-1))\nisreal(complex(1, 0))","after":"","output":"ans =\n\n  logical\n\n   1\n\nans =\n\n  logical\n\n   0\n\nans =\n\n  logical\n\n   0","figures":[]}],"notes":[],"see":["complex","real","isnumeric"]},{"names":["cplxpair"],"summary":"Sort complex numbers into conjugate pairs, the real ones last.","body":["The pairs come in order of increasing real part, the number with the negative imaginary part first in each; the real numbers follow, sorted. Numbers that have no conjugate are an error. Used to tidy roots and poles, which come in such pairs for real polynomials."],"syntax":[["B = cplxpair(A)","The numbers of `A` in conjugate pairs."],["B = cplxpair(A, tol)","Pairs within the tolerance `tol`."]],"inputs":[],"outputs":[],"examples":[{"title":"The roots of a real polynomial, paired","intro":"","code":"r = roots([1 3 4 8 2 6])';\ncplxpair(r)","after":"","output":"ans =\n\n  Columns 1 through 4\n\n   -0.3453 - 1.4816i   -0.3453 + 1.4816i    0.1681 - 0.9755i    0.1681 + 0.9755i\n\n  Column 5\n\n   -2.6455 + 0.0000i","figures":[]}],"notes":[],"see":["roots","sort"]},{"names":["unwrap"],"summary":"Remove the jumps of 2*pi from a sequence of phase angles.","body":["Angles from `angle` or `atan2` stay between -pi and pi, so a steadily turning phase jumps back by 2*pi each turn. `unwrap` adds multiples of 2*pi wherever two neighbours differ by more than pi (or `tol`), giving a smooth phase."],"syntax":[["q = unwrap(p)","The phases without their jumps."],["q = unwrap(p, tol)","With jumps larger than `tol` removed."],["q = unwrap(p, tol, dim)","Along dimension `dim`."]],"inputs":[],"outputs":[],"examples":[{"title":"A phase that turns three times","intro":"","code":"t = linspace(0, 3, 300);\np = angle(exp(1i*2*pi*t));\nplot(t, p, t, unwrap(p))\nlegend('angle', 'unwrapped', 'Location', 'northwest')","after":"","output":"","figures":[{"id":1,"type":"figure","number":1,"name":"","numberTitle":true,"width":560.0,"height":420.0,"color":[0.94,0.94,0.94],"visible":true,"version":6,"windowStyle":"normal","axes":[{"id":2,"type":"axes","kind":"cartesian","geo":false,"position":[0.13,0.11,0.775,0.815],"xlim":[0.0,3.0],"ylim":[-5.0,20.0],"zlim":[0.0,1.0],"xlimMode":"auto","ylimMode":"auto","zlimMode":"auto","clim":[0.0,1.0],"tight":{"X":false,"Y":false,"Z":false},"xscale":"linear","yscale":"linear","zscale":"linear","xdir":"normal","ydir":"normal","zdir":"normal","xgrid":false,"ygrid":false,"zgrid":false,"xminorgrid":false,"yminorgrid":false,"zminorgrid":false,"box":true,"color":[1.0,1.0,1.0],"xcolor":[0.15,0.15,0.15],"ycolor":[0.15,0.15,0.15],"zcolor":[0.15,0.15,0.15],"gridColor":[0.15,0.15,0.15],"gri